from core import L
L(5,'automata','Finite automata and regular expressions','Finite automata;DFA;NFA;Regular expressions','Sets, functions and strings.',
'Finite automata remember only which state they are in. A state should summarize exactly the past information needed to decide future acceptance. This viewpoint is more useful than memorizing transition drawings: design the meaning of states before adding edges.',
'''DFA and NFA semantics::A DFA has a total transition function Q×Σ→Q, one start state and a set of accepting states. Read the entire string before deciding acceptance. An NFA may have multiple possible transitions and epsilon moves; it accepts if at least one complete path ends accepting. Epsilon moves consume no symbol. Both models recognize exactly regular languages.
||Subset construction::A DFA state can represent a set of possible NFA states. Start with the epsilon closure of the NFA start. For a symbol, take all corresponding moves and then epsilon closure again. A subset is accepting if it contains an accepting NFA state. Up to 2ⁿ subsets exist for n NFA states, but many may be unreachable.
||Expressions and minimization::Regular expressions use union, concatenation and Kleene star; star includes the empty string. Precedence usually places star above concatenation above union. To minimize a DFA, remove unreachable states and distinguish accepting from nonaccepting states, then refine classes when transitions lead to different classes. States are equivalent only if no suffix distinguishes their acceptance.''',
'''Design a DFA for an even number of 1s over {0,1}::Use E and O to remember parity; start in E and accept E.~On 0, stay in the same state because parity does not change.~On 1, toggle E↔O. The empty string is accepted because zero is even.
||Interpret (0|1)*01::The starred prefix permits any binary string, including empty.~The final symbols are specifically 0 then 1.~The expression accepts exactly strings ending in 01, including 01 itself, but not 010.''',
'NFA acceptance is existential over paths, not all paths.||Complementing an incomplete DFA without a sink state can produce incorrect acceptance.',
'DFA and NFA have equal expressive power.||Subset construction has at most 2ⁿ candidate states.',
[('How many states are needed in the parity DFA described here?',2,'Remember even or odd.','The two classes E and O suffice and are distinguishable.'),('A 4-state NFA has how many possible subsets in subset construction?',16,'Count subsets of four states.','2⁴=16 possible subsets, not necessarily reachable.')])
L(5,'regular-pumping','Regular languages, closure and pumping lemma','Regular languages;Regular pumping lemma;Closure properties','Automata and quantified logic.',
'The pumping lemma captures a consequence of finite memory: a sufficiently long accepted run repeats a state, creating a loop. It is mainly a tool for proving nonregularity. The order of choices matters because you do not control how the opponent decomposes the string.',
'''Regular closure::Regular languages are closed under union, intersection, complement, difference, concatenation and star. Intersection can be recognized by a product automaton whose states pair the two machines' states. Complement requires a total DFA and flips accepting states. Closure can prove nonregularity indirectly by isolating a known difficult sublanguage.
||Pumping statement::If L is regular, some p exists so every w∈L with |w|≥p can be split w=xyz with |xy|≤p, |y|>0, and xyⁱz∈L for all integers i≥0. The repeated DFA segment is y. This is a necessary condition; merely finding pumpable decompositions does not establish regularity.
||Proof strategy::Assume regularity and let the opponent choose p. Choose a suitable w depending on p. Then handle every valid decomposition, not only one convenient y. Select an exponent i, often 0 or 2, that forces the pumped string out of L. Contradiction disproves the assumed regularity.''',
'''Prove {0ⁿ1ⁿ:n≥0} is not regular::Assume pumping length p and choose w=0ᵖ1ᵖ.~Because |xy|≤p and |y|>0, y consists of at least one zero and no ones.~Pump i=0: zeros are fewer than p while ones remain p, so the string leaves L. This works for every permitted split.
||Use a product DFA for two parity conditions::One machine remembers parity of zeros; the other parity of ones.~Pair their states to remember both parities, giving up to 2×2=4 states.~Accept only (even,even) to recognize the intersection.''',
'The opponent chooses the decomposition after your string choice.||Pumping is necessary, not sufficient, for regularity.',
'Pick w after p; handle all valid xyz splits.||Product automata combine regular constraints.',
[('The product of a 3-state and 4-state DFA has at most how many states?',12,'Pair every state from one with every state from the other.','3×4=12 candidate pairs.'),('For w=0⁵1⁵, a permitted regular-pumping y contains 2 zeros. After pumping i=0, how many zeros remain?',3,'Delete the y segment once.','5−2=3 zeros remain versus 5 ones.')])
L(5,'cfg-pda','Context-free grammars, derivations and pushdown automata','Context-free grammars;Push-down automata;Context-free languages;Ambiguity','Regular languages and recursion.',
'A stack lets a machine remember arbitrarily many pending obligations in last-in-first-out order. Context-free grammars describe recursive structure, and pushdown automata recognize the same language class. Nested parentheses and matched counts are natural examples.',
'''Grammar mechanics::A CFG has variables, terminals, a start variable and productions whose left side is one variable. Derivation replaces a variable using a production. A leftmost derivation always expands the leftmost variable; rightmost expands the rightmost. A parse tree records hierarchical structure, not the temporal order of independent expansions.
||PDA operation::A pushdown automaton chooses transitions using the current state, input symbol or epsilon, and stack top. For 0ⁿ1ⁿ, push one marker per 0, then pop one per 1 and accept when input and markers are exhausted. It must prevent returning to the zero phase after ones begin. Nondeterministic PDAs recognize all CFLs; deterministic PDAs recognize a proper subclass.
||Ambiguity and closure::A grammar is ambiguous if some string has two distinct parse trees, equivalently distinct leftmost derivations. A grammar can be changed while preserving its language. CFLs are closed under union, concatenation and star, and under intersection with a regular language; they are not closed under arbitrary intersection or complement.''',
'''Derive 0011 using S→0S1 | ε::Start S and apply 0S1 to get 0S1.~Apply again to get 00S11.~Use S→ε to obtain 0011; each recursive expansion adds a matching outer zero and one.
||Why E→E+E | E*E | id is ambiguous for id+id*id::One parse makes + the root and groups id+(id*id).~Another makes * the root and groups (id+id)*id.~Both follow the same productions but give different structures. Add precedence levels such as E,T,F to distinguish the intended grouping.''',
'Different arbitrary derivation orders do not necessarily imply different parse trees.||PDA determinism and nondeterminism differ in expressive power.',
'CFG left-hand sides contain one variable.||CFL ∩ regular is CFL; CFL ∩ CFL need not be.',
[('Using S→0S1|ε, how many nonempty recursive expansions produce 000111?',3,'One expansion adds one zero and one one.','Three applications of S→0S1 produce three matched pairs.'),('A PDA pushes one marker for each of 4 zeros. How many marker pops match four subsequent ones?',4,'One pop per one.','Four pushes are balanced by four pops.')])
L(5,'cfl-pumping','The context-free pumping lemma','Context-free pumping lemma;Non context-free languages','CFGs and regular pumping arguments.',
'Context-free pumping repeats two pieces because recursive grammar structure can surround a nested derivation. The proof pattern resembles regular pumping, but its decomposition has five parts and the two pumped parts may be separated.',
'''Statement and constraints::For a context-free language L, there exists p so every w∈L of length at least p can be written uvxyz with |vxy|≤p, |vy|>0, and uvⁱxyⁱz∈L for all i≥0. At least one of v,y is nonempty; neither individually must be nonempty. The bounded window vxy is important for controlling which symbol blocks can be changed.
||Proving non-CFL status::Assume L is context free, let p be arbitrary, and select a string depending on p. Show every valid decomposition fails for some pumping exponent. Often choose a string with three synchronized blocks so the bounded window cannot alter all required counts together. If pumping disturbs symbol ordering, the string also leaves the language.
||Limits of the method::Failure to find a useful contradiction is not a proof that a language is context free. Establish membership by constructing a grammar or PDA. Closure can simplify a difficult proof: intersecting with a regular language restricts patterns without leaving the CFL class if the original language truly is CFL.''',
'''Prove {aⁿbⁿcⁿ:n≥0} is not context free::Choose aᵖbᵖcᵖ after the supposed pumping length p.~The window vxy has length at most p, so it cannot span all three symbol blocks; pumping changes at most two block counts.~Because |vy|>0, i=0 removes some symbols while one block remains unchanged, or disrupts block order. Either way equal counts/order fail.
||Check the lemma conditions::A proposed split with both v=ε and y=ε has |vy|=0.~It cannot be used, regardless of x.~A split with v empty and y nonempty can be valid if the window bound holds.''',
'Use |vxy|≤p, not |uv|≤p as in regular pumping.||Both v and y are pumped by the same exponent.',
'CFL pumping: uvⁱxyⁱz, bounded middle window.||The lemma is a necessary property, not a membership algorithm.',
[('If |v|=2 and |y|=1, pumping from i=1 to i=3 adds how many symbols?',6,'Each extra repetition adds |v|+|y|.','Two extra repetitions add 2×(2+1)=6.'),('If |v|=0 and |y|=0, what is |vy|?',0,'Concatenation lengths add.','0+0=0, violating the required positive pumped length.')])
L(5,'decidability','Turing machines and undecidability','Turing machines;Undecidability;Halting problem;Reductions','Automata, proof by contradiction and algorithms.',
'A Turing machine adds an unbounded writable tape to finite control. It models general algorithmic computation. The crucial distinction is whether a machine always halts with an answer or may run forever on some negative instances.',
'''Deciders and recognizers::A decider halts on every input and accepts exactly its language. A recognizer accepts members but may reject or loop on nonmembers. Every decidable language is recognizable, but not conversely. If both a language and its complement have recognizers, interleave their steps; one must accept, giving a decider.
||The halting contradiction::Suppose H can decide whether any machine halts on an input. Construct D that on machine code x loops if H says machine x halts on x, and halts otherwise. Running D on its own code makes either prediction contradict what D does. Thus no general halting decider exists.
||Reductions and Rice::To show B undecidable, reduce a known undecidable A to B: transform A instances computably so a hypothetical B decider would decide A. Reversing the reduction proves the wrong implication. Rice’s theorem states every nontrivial property of the language recognized by a machine is undecidable; syntactic facts such as the number of states are not such semantic properties.''',
'''Turn two recognizers into a decider::Run one recognizer for L and one for its complement in alternating steps on the same input.~If the first accepts, answer yes; if the second accepts, answer no.~Every input belongs to exactly one side, so some recognizer eventually accepts. Running one to completion first could loop forever.
||Reason about a reduction A≤B::Assume a computable transform maps x∈A iff f(x)∈B.~If B had a decider, first compute f(x), then call it and return the same answer.~That would decide A. If A is undecidable, B cannot have such a decider.''',
'Recognizable does not mean decidable.||Reduction direction is essential.||Rice applies to semantic language properties, not all machine properties.',
'Decider halts everywhere; recognizer need not.||Known hard A≤B establishes hardness of B.',
[('A decider processes 20 inputs. How many of them may run forever?',0,'A decider must halt on every input.','Zero; any nonterminating input would violate the definition.'),('How many recognizers are interleaved in the language-and-complement decision construction?',2,'One for each side.','Two recognizers, one for L and one for its complement.')])
L(6,'lexical','Lexical analysis and tokenization','Lexical analysis;Tokens;Regular expressions;Longest match','Finite automata and C expressions.',
'The lexer groups characters into meaningful token classes before the parser checks grammar structure. A token records a category and possibly an attribute, while the lexeme is the actual source substring. Separating them lets a grammar treat every identifier similarly.',
'''Token definitions::Identifiers, keywords, numeric literals, operators and punctuation are common token classes. Regular expressions describe many lexical patterns; automata implement them. Whitespace and comments may be discarded or retained as metadata depending on the language. Symbol-table entries link identifier tokens to later declaration/type information.
||Longest match and priority::At each input position, a lexer typically chooses the longest matching lexeme. When matches have equal length, a rule priority resolves the tie, often recognizing a keyword before a generic identifier. Thus >= is one token rather than > followed by = if the token rule allows it. A keyword prefix inside an identifier does not force splitting: whilex can remain an identifier.
||Errors and boundaries::A lexical error is an unrecognized character sequence under the token rules. A valid token sequence can still have a parse error, such as misplaced parentheses. Nested structure is ordinarily handled by the parser, not by ordinary finite-automaton token rules. Scanner buffering must preserve character positions and handle end-of-input correctly.''',
'''Tokenize sum = a + 12;::sum is an identifier, = an assignment operator, and a another identifier.~+ is an arithmetic operator, 12 a numeric literal, and ; punctuation.~There are 6 tokens if whitespace is ignored; lexemes differ from their category names.
||Apply longest match to a>=10::Read a as an identifier.~At >, the two-character >= match is longer than the one-character > match.~Return identifier, >=, number: three tokens.''',
'Longest match beats an earlier shorter token rule.||Token category and source lexeme are different.',
'Lexer: characters→tokens.||Parser: tokens→grammatical structure.',
[('Ignoring whitespace, how many tokens are in x = 2 + 3 ; ?',6,'Count identifier, operators, literals and delimiter.','x,=,2,+,3,; gives 6 tokens.'),('With >= defined as an operator, how many tokens are in x>=5?',3,'Use longest match for the operator.','x,>=,5 gives 3 tokens.')])
L(6,'parsing','Parsing, FIRST/FOLLOW and LL/LR methods','Parsing;FIRST;FOLLOW;LL;LR','Context-free grammars and tokenization.',
'A parser discovers grammatical structure in a token stream. Predictive parsing chooses productions before seeing their full result; bottom-up parsing recognizes handles after seeing the right-hand side. FIRST and FOLLOW sets summarize where grammar decisions can be made.',
'''FIRST and FOLLOW::FIRST(α) contains terminals that can begin a string derived from α, plus ε if α can disappear. For a sequence, continue into later symbols when earlier ones are nullable. FOLLOW(A) contains terminals that can follow A in some sentential form; the start symbol includes end marker $. For A→αBβ, add FIRST(β) without ε to FOLLOW(B), and if β is nullable add FOLLOW(A).
||LL(1) prediction::For A→α, put the production under terminals in FIRST(α); if α is nullable, also under FOLLOW(A). A conflict means the grammar is not LL(1) as written. Eliminate direct left recursion and factor common prefixes when appropriate, without assuming those changes fix every grammar. Recursive descent expands a leftmost derivation.
||Bottom-up parsing::An LR parser tracks states representing viable prefixes, shifts input symbols and reduces a recognized right-hand side to its left-hand variable. Reductions reconstruct a rightmost derivation in reverse. Shift/reduce and reduce/reduce conflicts signal ambiguity or insufficient parsing information for the chosen method. LR handles a wider grammar class than LL(1).''',
'''For S→Ab and A→a|ε, compute FIRST(S)::A can begin with a or vanish.~If it vanishes, b is the first terminal of S.~FIRST(S)={a,b}; ε is absent because b cannot vanish.
||Build entries for A in that grammar::FIRST(a)={a}, so select A→a on lookahead a.~A→ε uses FOLLOW(A), which includes b from S→Ab.~Select A→ε on b. The entries are distinct and have no conflict.''',
'FOLLOW sets do not contain ε.||Nullable prefixes require inspecting subsequent symbols.',
'LL chooses a production by lookahead.||LR shifts and reduces viable prefixes.',
[('For S→Ab and A→a|ε, how many terminals are in FIRST(S)?',2,'Consider both taking and omitting A.','FIRST(S)={a,b}, size 2.'),('For A→aA|ε, how many symbols are in FIRST(A), including ε?',2,'A starts with a or disappears.','FIRST(A)={a,ε}, size 2.')])
L(6,'translation-runtime','Syntax-directed translation and runtime environments','Syntax-directed translation;Runtime environments;Attributes;Activation records','Parse trees, recursion and memory.',
'A parse tree can carry semantic information such as expression values or types. Syntax-directed translation describes how this information moves. Runtime environments then arrange the storage needed when the translated program actually executes.',
'''Attributes and evaluation::Synthesized attributes are computed from children and flow upward; inherited attributes receive information from a parent or relevant siblings. Semantic rules define dependencies, and a legal evaluation order respects that dependency graph. S-attributed definitions use only synthesized attributes and fit bottom-up evaluation naturally. L-attributed definitions restrict inherited dependencies for left-to-right evaluation.
||Translation actions::An annotated syntax tree can compute expression types, values, offsets or emitted code. For E→E₁+T, a synthesized value rule may set E.val=E₁.val+T.val. Translation schemes embed actions in productions, so action placement determines execution order during a derivation. Parsing correctness alone does not guarantee the semantic actions are correct.
||Runtime storage::An activation record can include arguments, return address, saved registers, locals and links. A dynamic link points to the caller’s frame; a static link supports lexical nesting and can point somewhere different. Stack allocation supports ordinary call-return lifetimes; heap allocation supports objects that outlive their creating call. Recursion needs separate frames even for the same function.''',
'''Evaluate attributes for expression 2+3*4::The multiplication node synthesizes 3×4=12.~The addition node receives child values 2 and 12.~It synthesizes 14; using a different parse tree could produce a different arithmetic order.
||Track calls main→f→f in recursion::main owns one frame; first f creates another.~Recursive f creates a third frame with its own locals.~Returning from inner f releases only its frame and restores the caller f’s state.''',
'Static and dynamic links serve different relationships.||Same function code does not imply shared local variables.',
'Synthesized flows upward; inherited receives context.||Activation records represent invocations, not functions as unique objects.',
[('For the parse tree 2+(3*4), what value is synthesized at the root?',14,'Evaluate the multiplication child first.','3×4=12, then 2+12=14.'),('main calls f, which calls f again. How many frames are active before any return, counting main?',3,'Count invocations rather than distinct names.','main, outer f and inner f give three frames.')])
L(6,'ir-dataflow','Intermediate code, local optimization and data-flow analysis','Intermediate code generation;Local optimisation;Constant propagation;Liveness analysis;Common sub expression elimination','Expressions, CFG control flow and sets.',
'Intermediate representations expose simple operations and control flow so optimizations can reason about program behaviour. An optimization must preserve observable semantics; matching expression text is insufficient when operands may have changed.',
'''Three-address code and blocks::Break compound expressions into assignments with at most a small fixed number of operands, such as t1=b*c; t2=a+t1. A basic block has one entry and no internal control-flow branch except at its end. Leaders include the first instruction, jump targets and instructions following jumps. A control-flow graph connects blocks by possible transfers.
||Local optimization::Constant folding evaluates constant expressions; constant propagation substitutes known constants. Common-subexpression elimination reuses a previously computed expression only if its operands and relevant memory remain unchanged. Dead-code elimination removes computations whose results have no future use, provided they have no required side effects. Overflow, aliasing and floating-point rules can constrain algebraic transformations.
||Data flow::Liveness is a backward may-analysis: OUT[B]=union of successors' IN sets; IN[B]=USE[B]∪(OUT[B]−DEF[B]). A variable is live if some continuation reads its current value before redefinition. Available expressions use forward propagation and typically intersection at joins because an expression must be available on every incoming path. Iterate finite-set equations to a fixed point.''',
'''Translate a=b+c*d::Emit t1=c*d because multiplication occurs first.~Emit t2=b+t1.~Emit a=t2. The temporaries make dependencies explicit for later analysis.
||Find live-in when USE={a}, DEF={b}, OUT={b,c}::Remove definitions from OUT: {b,c}−{b}={c}.~Union with USE: {a}∪{c}={a,c}.~Thus b’s old value is not needed on entry, but a and c are.''',
'Reusing a+b after assigning a is invalid without additional proof.||A may-analysis join uses union; must-properties often require intersection.',
'IN=USE∪(OUT−DEF) for liveness.||Preserve side effects when removing dead results.',
[('For USE={x}, DEF={y}, OUT={y,z}, how many variables are live-in?',2,'Apply the set equation.','IN={x}∪({y,z}−{y})={x,z}, size 2.'),('For t=4; u=t+6, what constant does propagation and folding assign to u?',10,'Substitute t before arithmetic.','u=4+6=10.')])
